@h1 Install h1@

Wit runs on Node. Most people want one thing: the @code wit code@ command, on
their machine, working. This page gets you there, proves it worked, and then
covers the paths for developers embedding Wit and contributors building from a
clone.

@h2 Before you start h2@

You need @strong Node 20 or newer strong@. Check what you have:

@@@pre
node --version
pre@@@

If that prints something below @code v20 code@, upgrade Node first — the tools
are built against modern Node and will not run on older versions. You only need
@strong pnpm 9+ strong@ if you plan to build Wit from a source clone (the last
section); to use Wit you do not.

@aside(class note) 💡 You don't have to install anything to try Wit. The
in-browser playground runs the same engine client-side, so you can experiment
before you commit to an install. aside@

@h2 The command-line tool (for writers) h2@

Install the CLI globally with npm:

@@@pre
npm install -g @witlang/cli
pre@@@

This gives you the @code wit code@ command everywhere. It has no runtime
dependencies, so the install is quick and clean.

@h2 Prove it works h2@

Two quick checks. First, the version:

@@@pre
wit --version
pre@@@

That prints @code 0.1.0 code@. Then the help, which is also your map of what the
tool can do:

@@@pre
wit --help
pre@@@

You'll see the full usage block:

@@@pre
wit v0.1.0

Usage:
  wit parse <file>
  wit check <file>
  wit fmt <file> [-w|--write]
  wit build <file> [-o out.html|out.md|out.pdf] [--format html|md|pdf] [--raw | --fragment]
  wit tour <file>
  wit --version | --help
pre@@@

Five subcommands. @code build code@ compiles a document; @code check code@
reports errors without building; @code fmt code@ tidies indentation;
@code parse code@ and @code tour code@ show you the internal structure. That is
the whole surface.

@h2 As project packages (for developers) h2@

If you're building @strong on strong@ Wit — parsing it inside your own app,
walking its typed tree, rendering it your own way — you want the libraries
rather than the CLI. Add them to a project:

@@@pre
npm install @witlang/parser @witlang/runtime @witlang/render-html @witlang/render-markdown
pre@@@

These are the same zero-dependency packages the CLI itself is built from. The
parser turns source into a tree, the runtime resolves and expands it, and the
render packages turn the result into HTML or Markdown. The
@a |href /docs/start/choose-your-path/| build path a@ picks up from here.

@h2 From a source clone (for contributors) h2@

To hack on Wit itself, clone the repository and build every package with pnpm:

@@@pre
pnpm install
pnpm build
pre@@@

There is no published binary in a clone, so you invoke the freshly built CLI by
its path:

@@@pre
node packages/cli/dist/bin.js build report.wit -o report.html
pre@@@

Everywhere these docs write @code wit … code@, a contributor working from a
clone reads @code node packages/cli/dist/bin.js … code@ — same tool, same flags.

@h2 The VS Code extension (optional) h2@

There is an editor extension with syntax highlighting and live diagnostics. It
is @strong not on the marketplace yet strong@ — you install it from a clone:

@@@pre
pnpm vscode:install
pre@@@

It needs VS Code 1.94 or newer and the @code code code@ command available on
your PATH. Marketplace publishing is on the roadmap; until then this is the way
in.

@h2 One more thing, for PDF h2@

Everything above is all you need for HTML and Markdown. @strong PDF output is
different strong@: Wit produces a PDF by paginating its HTML through a headless
copy of Chrome, so PDF needs a system Chrome or Chromium installed — or the
@code WIT_CHROME code@ environment variable pointed at a browser executable.
There is no bundled browser and nothing to download from Wit's side. If Chrome
isn't found, @code wit build code@ tells you so plainly rather than failing in
silence. The @a |href /docs/start/first-document/| first-document page a@ shows
the exact message and the fix.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Node too old. strong@ Below version 20, the tools won't run — upgrade Node, not Wit. li@
@li @strong Looking for the extension on the marketplace. strong@ It isn't there yet; install it from a clone with @code pnpm vscode:install code@. li@
@li @strong Thinking you must clone the repo to try Wit. strong@ You don't — the global CLI, or the in-browser playground, is enough. li@
@li @strong Expecting PDF to work with no browser. strong@ PDF needs a system Chrome or @code WIT_CHROME code@; HTML and Markdown need neither. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

With @code wit code@ installed, write
@a |href /docs/start/first-document/| your first document a@. If a command errors,
@a |href /docs/start/troubleshooting/| Troubleshooting a@ decodes it. To see where
Wit is headed — including the marketplace publish — read
@a |href /docs/start/what-is-wit/| What is Wit? a@
